#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24f2c5a5-dc56-5f78-8138-8f7b3a6280e2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4b9d89-3ff5-54da-820b-eb021d5fe3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297499} {:answer/id #uuid "42a00f97-17d6-5781-b3c2-c517e6a2a502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297500} {:answer/id #uuid "b0db2e2f-dd51-5285-bae9-7714789b537f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297501} {:answer/id #uuid "05648092-eeca-52af-ad86-0c572122b53f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297502} {:answer/id #uuid "d457b80a-0685-5505-9493-6872f3eabf07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d457b80a-0685-5505-9493-6872f3eabf07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297503} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d1bb207-997d-50fb-b711-479caefb0a09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db7eb34c-19dc-5bac-aefb-fd226ead4846" :question/math-academy-id "q-115053" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{9-2x}-3>-4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9-2x}-3 & >-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{9-2x} & >-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-1),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -2x & \\ge -9 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le \\frac{9}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8691714-11bf-5d6f-b408-dd725905dd83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297506} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e5f26a-8b86-5d1b-9905-5b8a2be8b11b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2<x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297507} {:answer/id #uuid "0bdc5ff1-eadc-511f-868d-7fbf1f58a122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297508} {:answer/id #uuid "24e9e9cb-b2b4-563e-9932-cfa325a61465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\le x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297509} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba67780-950f-5ca0-b6fe-c6874b19b240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8691714-11bf-5d6f-b408-dd725905dd83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297506} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c99caff-e9b9-51cb-9451-e28781324705" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e85dd46-e7c3-5897-aad1-f61ca46bc9a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-115060" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{x+2}-4>-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+2}-4 & >-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+2} & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{x+2})}^{2} & >{2}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & >4 \\\\ x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(2,\\infty ).$ The domain of the square root is $x\\ge -2,$ which can be expressed as $[-2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(2,\\infty )\\cap [-2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (2,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e544178-ab30-5a9a-ae0a-a927f900d259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e544178-ab30-5a9a-ae0a-a927f900d259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297513} :answer-field/id #uuid "45b7203a-834c-5df9-b9e7-443f4f3330cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297512} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f56d7949-c7ac-5bc6-8fee-6b5d103a6a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f56d7949-c7ac-5bc6-8fee-6b5d103a6a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297515} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff6e0618-95f6-54cc-8fba-b44f8e952f66" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2f0b8e2-445d-5900-a2e9-e7c3538ed57d" :question/math-academy-id "q-278283" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{3x-6}-4\\ge -1.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3x-6}-4 & \\ge -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x-6} & \\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(3).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{3x-6})}^{2} & \\ge {3}^{2} \\\\ 3x-6 & \\ge 9 \\\\ 3x & \\ge 15 \\\\ x & \\ge 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-6 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge 6 \\\\ x & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge 5,$ which can be expressed as $[5,\\infty ).$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge 2,$ which can be expressed as $[2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[5,\\infty )\\cap [2,\\infty )=[5,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [5,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $5\\le x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24f2c5a5-dc56-5f78-8138-8f7b3a6280e2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e117dc1-5bc2-5cd0-82e5-a4f3290a73e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297518} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d83820-b7e1-5686-86cd-a3a7f2348f82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297519} {:answer/id #uuid "815445c5-42d9-5b94-85cd-568d4132df13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297520} {:answer/id #uuid "544999d1-f565-59f8-840d-2c35a532561c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297521} {:answer/id #uuid "9b66266b-3b45-57e1-a600-9a877b6be59f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e117dc1-5bc2-5cd0-82e5-a4f3290a73e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297518} :answer-field/id #uuid "946a5e9d-53e3-5517-b1a8-45eccc61fcb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e440b43-c841-53b8-a07a-d961fcc2ab24" :question/math-academy-id "q-87681" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3-\\sqrt{x-1}<8.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\sqrt{x-1} & <8 \\\\ -\\sqrt{x-1} & <5 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-1} & >-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-5),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\nx-1\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24f2c5a5-dc56-5f78-8138-8f7b3a6280e2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f24499e-1383-57cf-8eae-e6fa2bb83692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297525} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b98672-3647-5ead-b442-45ec7ffa1a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297526} {:answer/id #uuid "e57c30e1-b45f-5d0b-94da-cefe0c6162e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297527} {:answer/id #uuid "b36b382f-8d51-53af-83b3-78bd61e8fead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297528} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0ea15f-f552-58c0-ac11-3db808bb4d05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e57c30e1-b45f-5d0b-94da-cefe0c6162e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297527} :answer-field/id #uuid "39ba0ef3-55c2-596e-9077-5c3996b511e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6794d77b-d5bc-52f7-8666-e16b9e0bff8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-87682" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4-3\\sqrt{x+3}\\le 10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-3\\sqrt{x+3} & \\le 10 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{x+3} & \\le 6 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+3} & \\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-2),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\nx+3\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge -3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -3.$"}]}
